## What was wrong

It wasn't a permissions or network problem — the `pip` **package directory had been deleted**, leaving only its metadata behind.

`site-packages` contained:

```
README.txt
pip-24.3.1.dist-info/     ← metadata claiming pip 24.3.1 is installed
```

…but no `pip/` directory. `RECORD` listed 841 files under `pip/`, and none of them existed. So `/usr/local/bin/pip` was a real launcher whose shebang pointed at a working `python3.13`, and it died on line 5:

```
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'
```

That's the confusing part of this failure mode: the `pip` command exists, the metadata says pip is installed, and `--version` still fails, because nothing can import it.

## The trap I hit first

The obvious repair, `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, **silently did nothing** and exited 0:

```
Requirement already satisfied: pip in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages (24.3.1)
```

`ensurepip` trusts the leftover `dist-info` and concludes pip is fine. The stale metadata has to be cleared before it will reinstall from the bundled wheel.

## The fix

The offline wheel `ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` was intact, so no network was needed:

1. Backed up the broken metadata to `/tmp/piptriage/`.
2. Removed `site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info` and the orphaned `/usr/local/bin/pip3.13`.
3. `python3 -m ensurepip --default-pip` → reinstalled pip 24.3.1 from the local wheel.

## Verification

`/tmp/verify_pip.sh` — I wrote it to fail if any of this regresses, and it passes end to end:

```
PASS  python3 -m pip --version
PASS  pip --version (bare)
PASS  pip3 --version
PASS  import pip
PASS  pip.__version__ == 24.3.1
PASS  pip submodules importable
PASS  site-packages/pip exists
PASS  no stale/missing-file state
PASS  pip list runs
PASS  pip can resolve a real install
PASS  file count matches RECORD (841)
---
ALL CHECKS PASSED
```

The last check is the one that matters most for your symptom: I did a genuine `pip install six` in a throwaway venv (`Successfully installed six-1.17.0`, then `import six` → `1.17.0`), and a `--dry-run` install against the network. Both work. `pip check` reports `No broken requirements found.`

## Two things worth noting

- **This was damage, not misconfiguration.** The directory was deleted at some point (note the site-packages mtime of Oct 31 vs the package files' Jan 14) — likely a manual `rm`, or an interrupted/interleaved install. It'll recur if whatever deleted it runs again; check any cleanup or image-pruning scripts that touch `site-packages`.
- **pip 24.3.1 is behind** (26.2.2 is current). I deliberately left it — you asked me to fix a broken install, not to change your package versions. `python3 -m pip install --upgrade pip` when you want it.

Backup of the broken metadata is at `/tmp/piptriage/` if you want to inspect it; delete it whenever. The script is at `/tmp/verify_pip.sh`.

